[Music]  John Carpenter and Deborah Hills  Halloween celebrates its 40th  anniversary in 2018 and the occasions  being marked with a new movie harkening  back to the original film the movie will  ignore all other sequels in the series  there have been 10 Halloween's since  1978 including all the sequels remakes  and detours are they really all worth  ignoring to answer that question were  strolling methodically through Halloween  history like Michael Myers starting with  the outings were most ready to forget  Halloween for the return of Michael  Myers the greatest sin a movie can  commit is being boring and the return of  Michael Myers is just that picking up  ten years after the original return  finds Michael waking up from a  decade-long coma when he overhears he  has a niece it didn't get much more  thrilling from there we learned that  Laurie Strode has died in a car accident  leaving a young daughter Jamie with  nothing better to do  Michael meanders into town to find her  the return of Michael Myers promptly  turns into a tepid retread as Michael  begins robotically mowing down teenagers  with flat cinematography giving the  uninspired action nowhere to hide it's a  movie that coasts on its own concept  ultimately going nowhere  Halloween 2007 though Rob Zombie was a  bold choice to spearhead this reboot the  result is an over complicated unpleasant  mess mr. zombie has no small amount of  style and he assembles a solid cast for  his reimagine unfortunately the  relentless dreariness and unstable story  structure make it hard to connect with  half lengthy backstory half a remake of  Halloween one this interesting effort  just doesn't add up  Halloween the curse of michael myers.slc  lot revolves around the cult of thorn a  druidic  order bent on gaining power through  ritual sacrifice their leader is  revealed to be dr. Terrence Wynn a  character who appeared in one scene of  the first Halloween that you certainly  won't remember the tangled drama leaves  little room for either scares or fun the  moe  compelling reason to watch as to marvel  at Paul Rudd who seemingly doesn't age  Halloween resurrection the ape Halloween  begins as a reality TV satire with  contestants being sent into the old  Myers house for a knife the house is  full of spooky traps but the danger gets  real when Michael unexpectedly comes  home the first thing he kills is the  interesting concept  watching Busta Rhymes yell at a computer  is extremely fun but even he can't save  resurrection from being a dreary slog as  an added insult the film opens with a  convoluted retcon to undo the ending of  Halloween h2o which was originally  intended as a series finale as part of  this Jamie Lee Curtis appears in a sad  cameo being brought back as a resident  of a mental hospital only to die again  Halloween to 2009 mr. zombie returns to  write and direct a sequel to his remake  and it's a marked improvement over the  first attempt zombie chose to shoot  Halloween 2 on 16-millimeter film  lending it an authentically gritty  grindhouse feel that sets it apart from  more glossy horror movies it helps make  the movie a surreal hallucinatory feast  a unique turn for the series even if the  resulting movie is still not great  Halloween 5 the revenge of Michael Myers  what the fifth Halloween may lack and  originality it makes up for with fun  since her last encounter with the shape  Jamie has been experiencing  hallucinations that suggest some kind of  telepathic link with her uncle Michael  the movie is full of 80s era indulgence  but the real attraction is Donald  Pleasence as dr. Loomis the  long-suffering nemesis of Michael Myers  going more over the top than ever in a  single-minded efforts to stop the killer  for good he fails Halloween to 1981  Halloween to continues the story of  Halloween as directly as possible  working desperately all the while to  feel different the sequel also explains  Michael's relationship to Laurie  who's revealed to be his long-lost  sister the filmmakers knew that the  mystique of Michael could never be  recaptured only continued the sequel is  set entirely on the same night as its  predecessor letting Myers loose in a  hospital for an outing that's much more  gory and violent than the one that came  to  or but the movie never really justifies  its own existence setting a standard for  all the unnecessary follow-ups to come  Halloween h2o Halloween h2o is one of  the silliest titles in horror history  with the series seventh movie having  nothing to do with water instead the  title refers to the 20 years that have  passed since the first two Halloween's  took place it reveals Laurie Strode to  be alive again living under an assumed  name after faking her death serving his  headmistress of a private school  attended by her teenage son h2o is a  Halloween for the screen generation with  strong late 90's vibes in its casting  and style but while those traits have an  age that well the movie itself has being  a tight slick thriller that lets Laurie  fight back when Michael comes for her  son it's Jamie Lee Curtis who makes h2o  worth watching not only is her  performance as a haunted middle-aged  lorry compelling the story was also her  idea  with Curtis pitching it as being about  regaining the soul by facing your own  fear hey isn't that what the new one's  about to Halloween 3 season of the witch  the boldest Halloween sequel tried its  hardest not to be a Halloween sequel  convinced that more Michael Myers would  lead to diminishing returns original  writers carpenter and Hill decided to  transform the franchise into an  anthology with a third film telling a  new story about Halloween night the  story is about an Ancient Order of  warlocks who plan to slaughter children  on Halloween using the popular masks of  an evil novelty company  the masks powered by microchips  containing pieces of Stonehenge cause  bugs and snakes to explode from the  heads of their wearers when they're  triggered by a special TV commercial the  attack is vengeance for the  commercialization of Halloween meant to  usher in a new age of witchcraft  while that weirdness is refreshing the  movie itself isn't quite as bonkers as  it sounds but it's full of memorable  moments and could have set up a whole  new direction for the series  unfortunately it was rejected by both  viewers and critics and the anthology  concept was abandoned  Halloween 1978 we can debate the merits  of every Halloween sequel but the  original is in a class of its own  Halloween became legendary by perfectly  executing a simple concept  it's characters are ordinary and  relatable and there's no contrived  motivations or mythology there's just a  man in a mask waiting to kill you  the waiting is the key to Halloween's  power there are surprisingly few murders  in the film and the killings that do  occur are almost bloodless the movie  mines horror from Dredd not gore leaving  you breathless once the climax kicks in  add in the shadowy cinematography and  carpenters creepy keyboard score and  you've got a minimalist masterpiece that  redefined a genre

